<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikita Khrushchev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1894">1894</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikita Khrushchev was leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from the mid-1950s to 1964, as first secretary of the Communist Party and, from 1958, also premier. His secret speech of 1956 denounced Stalin and began a partial thaw, while he crushed the Hungarian uprising and brought the world close to nuclear war in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a> of 1962. He was removed by his colleagues in 1964.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev.jpg" alt="Nikita Khrushchev" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TASS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1956_Press_Photo_Communist_Party_Secretary_Nikita_S._Khrushchev_in_Moscow_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nikita Sergeyevich Khrushchev was born on 15 April 1894 in the village of Kalinovka, in the Kursk Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, to poor peasant parents. His father worked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donbas">Donbas</a>, where wages were much higher, and left the family in Kalinovka between visits. Khrushchev worked as a metal worker in his youth and was a political commissar in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. In 1921 he was made assistant director for political affairs at the Rutchenkovo mine in the Donbas, and he had only four years of formal schooling.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under the patronage of Lazar Kaganovich, who became head of the Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 1925, Khrushchev rose quickly through the Soviet hierarchy. He expressed vehement support for the Moscow Trials of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a>, and in 1938 he was sent to govern the Ukrainian SSR, where he continued the purges. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he served as a political commissar and as an intermediary between Stalin and his commanders, and he was present at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stalingrad">Battle of Stalingrad</a>.</p><p>Recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, he headed the Party in Moscow city and province from December 1949. After Stalin's death on 6 March 1953 a power struggle followed, in which Khrushchev prevailed over rivals including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavrentiy-beria">Lavrentiy Beria</a> and was elected First Secretary of the Central Committee. On 25 February 1956 he delivered the Secret Speech at the 20th Party Congress, which denounced Stalin's purges and brought a less repressive era. His domestic policies, particularly in agriculture, were often ineffective. His years in office saw the tensest period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, culminating in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a> of 1962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Early in the 1960s his hold on power weakened because of his domestic failures and his handling of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a>. Leonid Brezhnev began plotting his removal in March 1964, and he was ousted on 14 October 1964. He received a pension and a house, fell into deep depression, and was treated as a non-person. His memoirs were smuggled to the West and published in part in 1970.</p><p>Khrushchev died of a heart attack on 11 September 1971, aged 77. He was denied a state funeral and was buried in the Novodevichy Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, with troops surrounding it. Many of his innovations were reversed after his fall. The historian Robert Service describes him as at once a Stalinist and an anti-Stalinist, a trouble-maker and a peacemaker.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nikita Khrushchev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nikita Khrushchev</b> denounced Stalin's crimes in his secret speech of 1956, presided over the release of most political prisoners from the Gulag and attempted reforms, but was removed in 1964.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nikita Khrushchev</b>, leader of the Soviet Union, denounced Stalin in the speech On the Cult of Personality and Its Consequences, a report to the 20th Congress of the Communist Party.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-25">25 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Recalled from holiday, <b>Nikita Khrushchev</b> was forced out by the Communist Party's Central Committee.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-14">14 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1954 <b>Nikita Khrushchev's</b> Virgin Lands campaign ploughed millions of hectares of steppe for wheat, bringing in Russian and Ukrainian settlers.</blockquote><a href="/state/kazakh-ssr">Kazakh SSR</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kalinovka, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.9,34.5&amp;year=1944" aria-label="Kalinovka on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5328 370 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nikita Khrushchev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5778 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5778 640h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.9,34.5&amp;year=1944">What was here in 1944? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
